     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Hi all….,</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">I thought you could find it interesting that I was able to call a Ruby method from Renjin, in a similar way that you call a Java method from Renjin.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">The details:</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">I have implemented the following interface in Java:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);border-radius:3px;display:inline;background-color:rgb(248,248,248);white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important\">public interface RubyCallbackInterface {\r\n\r\n    public Object run();\r\n    public Object run(Object obj1);\r\n    public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2);\r\n    public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3);\r\n    public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4);\r\n    public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4, Object obj5);\r\n    public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4, Object obj5, Object obj6);\r\n    public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4, Object obj5, Object obj6, Object obj7);\r\n\r\n}\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">I´ve implemented it like this because doing public Object run(Object… ) Renjin says there is no method that matches my run interface when I call it.  Seems to me like a bug. But for now this is working fine.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Then on the Ruby side I implement a class that implements this interface like so:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);border-radius:3px;display:inline;background-color:rgb(248,248,248);white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important\">class Callback &lt; Renjin::RubySexp\r\n  include Java::RbScicom.RubyCallbackInterface\r\n\r\n  attr_reader :ruby_obj\r\n\r\n  def initialize(ruby_obj)\r\n    @ruby_obj = ruby_obj\r\n  end\r\n\r\n  def run(*args)\r\n    method = args[0]\r\n    args.shift\r\n    @ruby_obj.send(method, *args)\r\n  end\r\n\r\n  def self.pack(obj, recursive: false)\r\n\r\n    if recursive\r\n      container = Array.new\r\n      obj.each do |pk|\r\n        container &lt;&lt; Callback.new(pk)\r\n      end\r\n      container\r\n    else\r\n      Callback.new(obj)\r\n    end\r\n\r\n  end\r\n\r\nend\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Basically what this code does, is create a class that has a ‘run’ method that receives a ‘name’ and a ‘list of arguments’.  The ‘name’ is the method name to be called and the argument list are the arguments to send to the method if it requires any.  The pack method can either pack a class or pack the content of the class if we have a container.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">This works fine.  So, lets now try it:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);border-radius:3px;display:inline;background-color:rgb(248,248,248);white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important\">  # create two Ruby arrays, props1 and props2\r\n  props1 = [1, 2, 3]\r\n  props2 = [1, 2]\r\n\r\n  # add the two  Ruby arrays into another Ruby array\r\n  props = [props1, props2]\r\n\r\n  # Now we &#39;pack&#39; our arrays inside a Callback class so that we can call method run on them from R\r\n  cb = R.rpack(props, recursive: true)\r\n\r\n  # Now we write an R script that call method.  #{cb.r} converts the cb Ruby variable to an R variable \r\n  R.eval(&quot;val &lt;- sapply(#{cb.r}, function(x) x$run(&#39;length&#39;))&quot;)\r\n  R.eval(&quot;print(val)&quot;)\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">This returns:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);border-radius:3px;display:inline;background-color:rgb(248,248,248);white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important\">  [1] 3 2\r\n</code></pre><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">This is really amazing!!!  </p>\r\n<div title=\"MDH:SGkgYWxsLi4uLiw8ZGl2Pjxicj48L2Rpdj48ZGl2PkkgdGhvdWdodCB5b3UgY291bGQgZmluZCBp\r\ndCBpbnRlcmVzdGluZyB0aGF0IEkgd2FzIGFibGUgdG8gY2FsbCBhIFJ1YnkgZnJvbSBSZW5qaW4s\r\nIGluIGEgc2ltaWxhciB3YXkgdGhhdCB5b3UgY2FsbCBhIEphdmEgbWV0aG9kIGZyb20gUmVuamlu\r\nLjwvZGl2PjxkaXY+PGJyPjwvZGl2PjxkaXY+PGJyPjwvZGl2PjxkaXY+VGhlIGRldGFpbHM6PC9k\r\naXY+PGRpdj48YnI+PC9kaXY+PGRpdj5JIGhhdmUgaW1wbGVtZW50ZWQgdGhlIGZvbGxvd2luZyBp\r\nbnRlcmZhY2UgaW4gSmF2YTo8L2Rpdj48ZGl2Pjxicj48L2Rpdj48ZGl2PjxkaXY+Jm5ic3A7ICZu\r\nYnNwOyBwdWJsaWMgaW50ZXJmYWNlIFJ1YnlDYWxsYmFja0ludGVyZmFjZSB7PC9kaXY+PGRpdj48\r\nYnI+PC9kaXY+PGRpdj4mbmJzcDsgJm5ic3A7ICZuYnNwOyAmbmJzcDsgcHVibGljIE9iamVjdCBy\r\ndW4oKTs8L2Rpdj48ZGl2PiZuYnNwOyAmbmJzcDsgJm5ic3A7