     1,
     "Nice!\r<br>\r<br>Would be interested to correct the problem with varArgs methods... do\r<br>you have a simple example we could include as a test?\r<br>\r<br>VarArg does seem to be handled here:\r<br><a href=\"https://github.com/bedatadriven/renjin/blob/master/core/src/main/java/org/renjin/invoke/reflection/AbstractOverload.java#L83\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/bedatadriven/renjin/blob/master/core/src/main/java/org/renjin/invoke/reflection/AbstractOverload.java%23L83&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793343768085000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0PfCtvd27hGMepNVmZUEEr\">https://github.com/bedatadriven/renjin/blob/master/core/src/main/java/org/renjin/invoke/reflection/AbstractOverload.java#L83</a>\r<br>\r<br>But something must be going wrong...\r<br>\r<br>Alex\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Tue, Feb 16, 2016 at 5:28 PM, Rodrigo Botafogo\r<br>&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">rod...@rodrigobotafogo.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi all….,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I thought you could find it interesting that I was able to call a Ruby\r<br>&gt; method from Renjin, in a similar way that you call a Java method from\r<br>&gt; Renjin.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; The details:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I have implemented the following interface in Java:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; public interface RubyCallbackInterface {\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     public Object run();\r<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1);\r<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2);\r<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3);\r<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4);\r<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4,\r<br>&gt; Object obj5);\r<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4,\r<br>&gt; Object obj5, Object obj6);\r<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4,\r<br>&gt; Object obj5, Object obj6, Object obj7);\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I´ve implemented it like this because doing public Object run(Object… )\r<br>&gt; Renjin says there is no method that matches my run interface when I call it.\r<br>&gt; Seems to me like a bug. But for now this is working fine.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Then on the Ruby side I implement a class that implements this interface\r<br>&gt; like so:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; class Callback &lt; Renjin::RubySexp\r<br>&gt;   include Java::RbScicom.RubyCallbackInterface\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   attr_reader :ruby_obj\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   def initialize(ruby_obj)\r<br>&gt;     @ruby_obj = ruby_obj\r<br>&gt;   end\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   def run(*args)\r<br>&gt;     method = args[0]\r<br>&gt;     args.shift\r<br>&gt;     @ruby_obj.send(method, *args)\r<br>&gt;   end\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   def self.pack(obj, recursive: false)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     if recursive\r<br>&gt;       container = Array.new\r<br>&gt;       obj.each do |pk|\r<br>&gt;         container &lt;&lt; Callback.new(pk)\r<br>&gt;       end\r<br>&gt;       container\r<br>&gt;     else\r<br>&gt;       Callback.new(obj)\r<br>&gt;     end\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   end\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; end\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Basically what this code does, is create a class that has a ‘run’ method\r<br>&gt; that receives a ‘name’ and a ‘list of arguments’. The ‘name’ is the method\r<br>&gt; name to be called and the argument list are the arguments to send to the\r<br>&gt; method if it requires any.  The pack method can either pack a class or pack\r<br>&gt; the content of the class if we have a container.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; This works fine. So, lets now try it:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   # create two Ruby arrays, props1 and props2\r<br>&gt;   props1 = [1, 2, 3]\r<br>&gt;   props2 = [1, 2]\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   # add the two  Ruby arrays into another Ruby array\r<br>&gt;   props = [props1, props2]\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   # Now we &#39;pack&#39; our arrays inside a Callback class so that we can call\r<br>&gt; method run on them from R\r<br>&gt;   cb = R.rpack(props, recursive: true)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   # Now we write an R script that call method.  #{cb.r} converts the cb Ruby\r<br>&gt; variable to an R variable\r<br>&gt;   R.eval(&quot;val &lt;- sapply(#{cb.r}, function(x) x$run(&#39;length&#39;))&quot;)\r<br>&gt;   R.eval(&quot;print(val)&quot;)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; This returns:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;   [1] 3 2\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; This is really amazing!!!\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; --\r<br>&gt; Rodrigo Botafogo\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
