     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Em quarta-feira, 17 de fevereiro de 2016 05:33:38 UTC-2, Alexander Bertram  escreveu:</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\"></p><div><p></p><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">Nice!\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Would be interested to correct the problem with varArgs methods... do\r\n<br>you have a simple example we could include as a test?\r\n<br>\r\n<br>VarArg does seem to be handled here:\r\n<br><a href=\"https://github.com/bedatadriven/renjin/blob/master/core/src/main/java/org/renjin/invoke/reflection/AbstractOverload.java#L83\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/bedatadriven/renjin/blob/master/core/src/main/java/org/renjin/invoke/reflection/AbstractOverload.java%23L83&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793343768088000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3cMcVb6uG7qBcvmSpn4hnx\">https://github.com/bedatadriven/renjin/blob/master/core/src/main/java/org/renjin/invoke/reflection/AbstractOverload.java#L83</a>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>But something must be going wrong...\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Alex\r\n<br>\r\n<br>On Tue, Feb 16, 2016 at 5:28 PM, Rodrigo Botafogo\r\n<br>&lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">rod...@rodrigobotafogo.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r\n<br>&gt; Hi all….,\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; I thought you could find it interesting that I was able to call a Ruby\r\n<br>&gt; method from Renjin, in a similar way that you call a Java method from\r\n<br>&gt; Renjin.\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; The details:\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; I have implemented the following interface in Java:\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; public interface RubyCallbackInterface {\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;     public Object run();\r\n<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1);\r\n<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2);\r\n<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3);\r\n<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4);\r\n<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4,\r\n<br>&gt; Object obj5);\r\n<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4,\r\n<br>&gt; Object obj5, Object obj6);\r\n<br>&gt;     public Object run(Object obj1, Object obj2, Object obj3, Object obj4,\r\n<br>&gt; Object obj5, Object obj6, Object obj7);\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; }\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; I´ve implemented it like this because doing public Object run(Object… )\r\n<br>&gt; Renjin says there is no method that matches my run interface when I call it.\r\n<br>&gt; Seems to me like a bug. But for now this is working fine.\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; Then on the Ruby side I implement a class that implements this interface\r\n<br>&gt; like so:\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; class Callback &lt; Renjin::RubySexp\r\n<br>&gt;   include Java::RbScicom.RubyCallbackInterface\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;   attr_reader :ruby_obj\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;   def initialize(ruby_obj)\r\n<br>&gt;     @ruby_obj = ruby_obj\r\n<br>&gt;   end\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;   def run(*args)\r\n<br>&gt;     method = args[0]\r\n<br>&gt;     args.shift\r\n<br>&gt;     @ruby_obj.send(method, *args)\r\n<br>&gt;   end\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;   def self.pack(obj, recursive: false)\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;     if recursive\r\n<br>&gt;       container = Array.new\r\n<br>&gt;       obj.each do |pk|\r\n<br>&gt;         container &lt;&lt; Callback.new(pk)\r\n<br>&gt;       end\r\n<br>&gt;       container\r\n<br>&gt;     else\r\n<br>&gt;       Callback.new(obj)\r\n<br>&gt;     end\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;   end\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; end\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; Basically what this code does, is create a class that has a ‘run’ method\r\n<br>&gt; that receives a ‘name’ and a ‘list of arguments’. The ‘name’ is the method\r\n<br>&gt; name to be called and the argument list are the arguments to send to the\r\n<br>&gt; method if it requires any.  The pack method can either pack a class or pack\r\n<br>&gt; the content of the class if we have a container.\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; This works fine. So, lets now try it:\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;   # create two Ruby arrays, props1 and props2\r\n<br>&gt;   props1 = [1, 2, 3]\r\n<br>&gt;   props2 = [1, 2]\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;   # add the two  Ruby arrays into another Ruby array\r\n<br>&gt;   props = [props1, props2]\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;   # Now we &#39;pack&#39; our arrays inside a Callback class so that we can call\r\n<br>&gt; method run on them from R\r\n<br>&gt;   cb = R.rpack(props, recursive: true)\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;   # Now we write an R script that call method.  #{cb.r} converts the cb Ruby\r\n<br>&gt; variable to an R variable\r\n<br>&gt;   R.eval(&quot;val &lt;- sapply(#{cb.r}, function(x) x$run(&#39;length&#39;))&quot;)\r\n<br>&gt;   R.eval(&quot;print(val)&quot;)\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; This returns:\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;   [1] 3 2\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; This is really amazing!!!\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; --\r\n<br>&gt; Rodrigo Botafogo\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt;\r\n<br>&gt; --\r\n<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups\r\n<br>&gt; &quot;Renjin Contributors&quot; group.\r\n<br>&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an\r\n<br>&gt; email to <a rel=\"nofollow\">renjin-dev+...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r\n<br>&gt; For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793343768088000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3N1MrGdFsZE67aco12cK1u\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>-- \r\n<br>Alex Bertram\r\n<br>Technical Director\r\n<br>bedatadriven\r\n<br>\r\n<br>Web: <a href=\"http://bedatadriven.com\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&